<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Deliberately empty. Vigil claims no entitlements.

       Nothing here needs one. Not sandboxed: we drive IOKit power assertions
       and talk to a local socket that CLI hooks write to, and sandboxing would
       break both. But "not sandboxed" was never the reason this file could
       safely carry com.apple.security.automation.apple-events set to false.
       That key belongs to the Hardened Runtime as well as to the sandbox
       (Apple files it under Resource Access and tells you to reach it by
       enabling the Hardened Runtime capability first), and bundle.sh signs
       this app with the hardened runtime on. It was never inert here.

       Removed rather than corrected, because Apple's instruction to anyone
       signing by hand is explicit: "The default value of these Boolean
       entitlements is false. When Xcode signs your code, it includes an
       entitlement only if the value is true. If you're manually signing code,
       follow this convention to ensure maximum compatibility. Don't include
       an entitlement if the value is false."
       https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/hardened-runtime

       Absent and present-but-false are not the same thing to every checker
       that reads a signature, and the first notarised build is the wrong place
       to find out which. The one caller that could have cared is
       ClamshellInstaller, which drives NSAppleScript; the value it would ever
       want is true, never false, and it needs neither. Apple again: "Your app
       doesn't need the Apple Events entitlement if it only sends Apple events
       to itself or to other processes signed with the same team ID."
       https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.security.automation.apple-events

       So: if something here ever genuinely needs an entitlement, add it with
       the value true. Never add one set to false in order to record a
       decision. That is what this comment is for.

       (Keep this comment free of double hyphens and of angle brackets. The
       parser codesign hands it to is not a full XML parser and rejects both,
       with a line number and no explanation.) -->
</dict>
</plist>
